# cross_vmnet_pf_override.pf — open routed IP traffic between the two UTM
# vmnet-shared subnets on the macOS lab host (CP-1).
#
# WHY: the host already forwards (net.inet.ip.forwarding=1) and owns both
# gateways (192.168.64.1 and 192.168.65.1 on two bridgeN interfaces), but
# vmnet loads the pf anchor com.apple.internet-sharing/network_isolation with
#   block drop quick inet from <network_isolation_table_v4> to <network_isolation_table_v4>
# and both lab /24s sit in that table, so every guest<->guest packet across
# the split is dropped on the host. The macOS guest (Apple Virtualization
# backend) lands on one subnet and the QEMU guests on the other by design
# (VmLabNetworkStandard.md), so any macOS<->Linux data-path stage needs this.
#
# HOW IT WINS: /etc/pf.conf evaluates `anchor "com.apple/*"` BEFORE the
# internet-sharing anchor, and wildcard sub-anchors run in sorted order, so a
# `pass quick` rule loaded under com.apple/100.rustynet-lab matches first.
# The rules are symmetric across bridge100/bridge101 because UTM swaps the
# bridge numbering between reboots.
#
# RUNTIME-ONLY: the anchor is gone after a host reboot (and possibly after a
# vmnet restart). Re-load before any macOS<->Linux cross-vmnet run:
#   sudo pfctl -a com.apple/100.rustynet-lab -f scripts/vm_lab/cross_vmnet_pf_override.pf
# Verify:
#   sudo pfctl -a com.apple/100.rustynet-lab -sr
# Undo (do NOT run this right after the load — it silently re-blocks):
#   sudo pfctl -a com.apple/100.rustynet-lab -F all
# The "-f ... could result in flushing of rules" warning refers to the main
# ruleset; loading into a sub-anchor leaves it alone.
#
# Full write-up, evidence and caveats (L3-routed, not same-L2; broadcast/mDNS
# does not cross): documents/operations/active/MacosCrossNetworkTrafficBlocker_2026-09-03.md §9.
pass quick on bridge101 inet from 192.168.64.0/24 to 192.168.65.0/24 flags any keep state
pass quick on bridge100 inet from 192.168.64.0/24 to 192.168.65.0/24 flags any keep state
pass quick on bridge100 inet from 192.168.65.0/24 to 192.168.64.0/24 flags any keep state
pass quick on bridge101 inet from 192.168.65.0/24 to 192.168.64.0/24 flags any keep state
